course: religion-ethics
lesson: 14
title: Parables: the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son and the Lost Sheep
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A parable is a short story Jesus told about everyday life with a deeper meaning about God and how to live; it makes the listener think and see himself in it.
explain: When a lawyer asked «Who is my neighbour?», Jesus told of a man robbed and left half dead: a priest and a Levite passed by on the other side, but a Samaritan — from a people the listeners disliked — stopped, bandaged him and paid for his care. Jesus ended: «Go and do likewise».
explain: In the Prodigal Son a younger son wastes his share of his father's money and comes home ashamed; the father runs to welcome him, while the elder brother sulks at the party.
explain: In the Lost Sheep a shepherd leaves ninety-nine sheep to find one, and in the Lost Coin a woman sweeps her house for one silver coin; both celebrate when they find it.
explain: Christians believe these stories show that God welcomes anyone who turns back (repentance), and that a neighbour is anyone in need — even someone from a group you were taught to dislike.
fact: A parable is a short story about everyday life with a deeper meaning about God and how to live.
fact: The Good Samaritan was Jesus' answer to a lawyer's question «Who is my neighbour?»; «love your neighbour as yourself» was already a command in the Jewish law of the Hebrew Bible, which the lawyer knew.
fact: In the parable a man was robbed and left half dead; a priest and a Levite, a helper in the Temple, passed by on the other side of the road.
fact: A Samaritan, from a people many of Jesus' listeners disliked, stopped, cared for the man and paid an innkeeper to look after him.
fact: Jesus turned the question into «Who acted as a neighbour?» and ended with «Go and do likewise».
fact: In the Prodigal Son a younger son took his share of his father's money, wasted it, and came home ashamed.
fact: The father ran to welcome the younger son and threw a feast for him.
fact: The elder brother, who had stayed at home, was angry that the father celebrated; the father told him «everything I have is yours» and that they had to celebrate because his brother was lost and is found.
fact: In the Lost Sheep a shepherd left ninety-nine sheep to search for one that was lost, and rejoiced when he found it.
fact: In the Lost Coin a woman searched her whole house for one lost silver coin and called her friends to celebrate when she found it.
fact: Jesus said there is joy in heaven over one person who turns back.
fact: Repentance means turning back: being sorry and changing direction.
exercise: What is a parable? | A short everyday story with a deeper meaning | A true report of something that really happened long ago | A song of praise sung in the Temple | A law written on tablets of stone
exercise: Why did Jesus tell the Good Samaritan? | A lawyer asked him «Who is my neighbour?» | The disciples asked how to pray | A Samaritan asked whether he could join the apostles | The priests asked about the Sabbath
exercise: Who passed by the hurt man without helping? | A priest and a Levite | A Samaritan and an innkeeper | Two Roman soldiers | Two fishermen from Galilee
exercise: What did the Samaritan do for the hurt man? | Bandaged him and paid for his care at an inn | Gave him directions and walked on | Fetched a priest to come and help | Chased the robbers to get the money back
exercise: Why was it surprising that the helper was a Samaritan? | Many of Jesus' listeners disliked Samaritans | Samaritans were all trained doctors in those days | Samaritans were not allowed on roads | The Samaritan was the hurt man's brother
exercise: What does «passing by on the other side» mean today? | Making excuses to avoid helping someone | Crossing the road safely at a crossing | Choosing a different way home from school | Helping someone from across the street
exercise: How did Jesus end the Good Samaritan? | Go and do likewise | Love your enemies and pray | Blessed are the poor | Let the children come to me
exercise: Who is our neighbour, according to the parable? | Anyone in need, even someone from a disliked group | Only the people who live next door or down our street | Only people of our own faith | Only people who have helped us first
exercise: What did the younger son do with his share of the money? | Wasted it far from home | Gave it all to the poor | Buried it in the ground | Used it to buy a farm
exercise: How did the father react when the younger son came home? | He ran to welcome him and held a feast | He made him work as a servant forever | He sent him away to his brother | He waited to hear him beg first
exercise: Why was the elder brother angry? | His father celebrated the brother who had wasted everything | His father gave him less food at the table than the servants | His younger brother stole his room | His father sold the farm
exercise: What did the shepherd do when one sheep was lost? | Left the ninety-nine to search for the one | Decided ninety-nine was enough and went home happy | Bought a new sheep at the market the next morning | Sent his dog out to look and went off to sleep
exercise: What did the woman search for in the Lost Coin? | One silver coin | A lost ring of gold | Her missing sheep | A key to the house
exercise: What do the Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin both end with? | A celebration with friends | A punishment for carelessness | The thing staying lost forever | A lesson about saving money
exercise: What does repentance mean? | Turning back: being sorry and changing direction | Saying sorry out loud but then doing it all again | Being punished for a wrong | Forgetting about what you did
exercise: What do Christians believe the Prodigal Son shows about God? | God is like a father who forgives and welcomes back | God never forgives anyone who wastes things | God loves only those who never go wrong | God cares about money above all
test: bro: «the Good Samaritan was a priest who helped». nah? | No, the priest passed by; the Samaritan helped | Yes, the priest stopped and helped him | No, it was a soldier who helped | Yes, the Levite and the priest helped together
test: Told today, who might the Samaritan be? | Someone from a group your town is taught to dislike | The vicar of your own church | Your best friend from class | A famous footballer that everyone in town already loves
test: Someone's crying in the corridor, mate says «not our problem, we'll be late, move». Which choice matches «go and do likewise»? | Stop and check on them, then get help if needed | Walk past fast so you're not late | Film it to show the group chat | Tell them to stop being a baby
test: A girl who wrecked the class project says sorry and wants back in. Your friend: «no way, she's a total idiot». Best-reasoned answer? | Let her back: she's sorry, and we'd want the same chance | No, because she's an idiot like he says | No, because once wrong means always wrong | Yes, but only if she agrees to do all the rest of the work alone
test: Elder sibling: «I've been good for years and the screw-up gets the party? so unfair». What did the father say? | All I have is yours; we celebrate because your brother was lost and is found | You are right, I will cancel the party now | Your brother is my favourite, so be quiet | Go away, you never helped me anyway
test: Classmate with no religion asks: «Why leave 99 sheep for 1? that's bad maths». Best answer? | The story shows every single person matters to God | Shepherds always lost more sheep than they ever found again | The 99 were already sold at the market | The one sheep was made of gold
test: Jewish classmate: «Wasn't the lawyer in the story asking a real question from our law?» Best reply? | Yes, «love your neighbour» is in the Hebrew Bible too | No, Jews have no rule about neighbours | No, the lawyer was a Roman judge | Yes, but Jesus said that part of the law was wrong and old
test: «Repent» sounds scary, says Lily. What does it actually mean? | Turning around: being sorry and changing your way | Being punished in front of everyone | Feeling bad forever without stopping | Saying the sorry words out loud but changing nothing at all
test: Judge: «Why did Jesus answer the lawyer with a story instead of a rule?» | A story makes him see who acted as neighbour | Jesus did not know the answer to the question | Stories were the only thing allowed to be taught | Rules about who counts as a neighbour did not exist then
test: Which two parables both end with a party after finding something? | The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin | The Good Samaritan and the Lost Coin | The Lost Sheep and the Good Samaritan | The Prodigal Son and the Good Samaritan
test: What excuse might the priest have had for passing by, and was it good? | Fear or being busy, but the man's need was greater | He had to feed his sheep, which was more important | He was not allowed on that road at all | He did not see the man because it was dark
exam: Identify the principal question which occasioned the parable of the Good Samaritan. | «Who is my neighbour?» | «How ought we to pray?» | «Who is the greatest?» | «Is it lawful to pay taxes?»
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of the father in the parable of the Prodigal Son? | He ran to receive his returning son and ordered a feast | He consented to receive him solely as a hired servant | He declined to receive him until restitution was made | He remained indifferent to his son's return
exam: Ascertain the number of sheep left by the shepherd in order that the lost one might be sought. | Ninety-nine | Ninety | One hundred | Twelve
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the term «repentance». | A turning back, comprising sorrow and a change of direction | The endurance of a penalty imposed by a court | A formal apology unaccompanied by any change | The cessation of all thought concerning past deeds
